If the segregation distortion is real, then you can just ignore it. QTL mapping conditions on the observed genotypes, and so distortion has no real influence on the assessment of genotype:phenotype association, except in maybe affecting power to detect QTL, if the sample size for some genotypes are low.

So the main question is: is this real, or is it some problem in the genotyping technology? The main clue is whether linked markers along a chromosome are all showing smoothly varying allele frequencies. If there's a lot of scatter, I'd suspect genotyping errors.

karl

